  <small>AUTHOR BIOGRAPHY</small>

  <h2>Maximo — The Waiting-Maximo</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Maximo</strong> is a Zambian writer, learner and
    creative thinker whose work explores the realities of
    growing through pressure, uncertainty, responsibility,
    disappointment, ambition, faith and hope.
  </p>

  <p>
    Writing under the name <strong>The Waiting-Maximo</strong>,
    he gives words to experiences that many people carry quietly.
    His writing is reflective, direct and grounded in real life,
    with a focus on endurance, personal growth and the courage
    to continue when the future is not yet clear.
  </p>

  <p>
    His work is written for people who are still becoming:
    people who may be tired but are still trying, people building
    a future while dealing with present responsibilities, and
    people learning that a painful season does not have to
    become the ending.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    “We may not choose every chapter life gives us,
    but we can choose how we write the next one.”
  </blockquote>


  <h2>About The Waiting-Maximo</h2>

  <p>
    The name <strong>The Waiting-Maximo</strong> represents
    patience while becoming, endurance while building, and
    faith while the future is still unknown.
  </p>

  <p>
    Waiting is not surrender. Sometimes waiting means preparing,
    learning, growing and becoming ready for what comes next.
  </p>

  <p>
    The identity grew from a simple conviction:
    <strong>the story is not finished just because
    the present chapter is difficult.</strong>
  </p>


  <h2>Why I Write</h2>

  <p>
    I write about the things people often carry silently —
    pressure, loneliness, sacrifice, disappointment, ambition,
    failure, faith and hope.
  </p>

  <p>
    Some of the strongest people you meet are fighting battles
    nobody can see. A smile does not always mean that everything
    is fine. Sometimes a person simply chooses to keep moving.
  </p>

  <p>
    My writing is not meant to pretend that life is easy.
    It is meant to remind the reader that pain can be real
    without becoming the whole story.
  </p>


  <h2>Writing &amp; Works</h2>

  <p>
    <em>The Pain We Carry</em> is one of the central works
    of The Waiting-Maximo.
  </p>

  <p>
    Other developing writings include:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><em>The Limitless</em></li>
    <li><em>The Irreplaceable</em></li>
    <li><em>Stepping into the Unknown</em></li>
    <li><em>Grown as a Soldier</em></li>
    <li><em>The Waiting-Maximo</em></li>
  </ul>


  <h2>Faith &amp; Perspective</h2>

  <p>
    Faith in Jehovah God is part of the foundation of my hope.
    I do not believe faith means that life will never hurt.
  </p>

  <p>
    I believe faith can help a person keep perspective,
    endure difficult seasons and continue moving forward
    when circumstances are painful.
  </p>


  <h2>To the Reader</h2>

  <p>
    If you found this book because you are carrying something
    nobody else understands, I hope these pages give you
    a place to breathe.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you are waiting for your life to change,
    keep preparing while you wait.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you have fallen, get back up when you can.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you are tired, rest — but remember that rest and
    quitting are not the same thing.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <strong>
      You are allowed to feel the pain.
      Just don't let the pain write your whole story.
    </strong>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    <em>
      Still waiting. Still learning.
      Still becoming. Still writing.
    </em>
  </p>

  <p class="signature">
    — The Waiting-Maximo
  </p>

